WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:12.309
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: May we move on to you, Paul,
for the finance report, including the NHS

00:00:12.309 --> 00:00:13.309
reset?

00:00:13.309 --> 00:00:14.870
&gt;&gt;Paul Baumann: Thank you, Malcolm.

00:00:14.870 --> 00:00:17.820
So in my constant attempt to bring you value
for money, I am offering you four reports

00:00:17.820 --> 00:00:20.740
in one in this particular item.

00:00:20.740 --> 00:00:25.640
I start by summarising the final outcome of
the planning process for 2016/17 with regard

00:00:25.640 --> 00:00:26.900
to the commissioning sector.

00:00:26.900 --> 00:00:31.720
We go on to talk about financial performance
and the forecast as at month three; hot off

00:00:31.720 --> 00:00:36.350
the presses, I would say, thanks to the accelerated
reporting process that we have been able to

00:00:36.350 --> 00:00:38.510
introduce in 2016/17.

00:00:38.510 --> 00:00:43.520
I have highlighted in the report the risks
to that position in a year of significant

00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:48.079
challenge for the NHS as a whole, and no less
for the commissioning sector.

00:00:48.079 --> 00:00:52.710
Finally, I have just drawn to the board's
attention the financial reset document which

00:00:52.710 --> 00:00:56.210
we published last week along with our report
and accounts.

00:00:56.210 --> 00:01:02.170
So just briefly turning to each of those topics,
you can see the key elements of the plan set

00:01:02.170 --> 00:01:04.019
out in the first section of the report.

00:01:04.019 --> 00:01:08.750
I just want to draw your attention to four
key aspects of the plan.

00:01:08.750 --> 00:01:13.369
The first and possibly most important is that
it is actually balanced, and I would say we

00:01:13.369 --> 00:01:20.070
are committed to living within the £106.5
billion of resource made available to us for

00:01:20.070 --> 00:01:21.829
the current year.

00:01:21.829 --> 00:01:24.890
Second thing: it is extremely tight.

00:01:24.890 --> 00:01:30.869
When provider deficit funding and pensions
effect are stripped out, we have 0.4% per

00:01:30.869 --> 00:01:35.210
head of population to cover all of the rest
of the pressures on our core commissioning

00:01:35.210 --> 00:01:41.180
budget; plus, of course - and this is an important
plus - the headroom that we create through

00:01:41.180 --> 00:01:45.640
the efficiency programmes that we are running
during the course of the year.

00:01:45.640 --> 00:01:52.680
Third point is, we have carved out 1% of allocations
as an investment reserve, to fulfil two primary

00:01:52.680 --> 00:01:53.980
functions.

00:01:53.980 --> 00:01:58.259
The first is to act as a buffer for any over
spends in either the commissioning or the

00:01:58.259 --> 00:02:03.310
provider sectors, in what is, as I have said,
a year of exceptional financial challenge.

00:02:03.310 --> 00:02:08.530
To the extent that such over spends arise,
we will need to release the reserve to commissioners'

00:02:08.530 --> 00:02:13.701
bottom lines to offset the resulting deficit
elsewhere in the system.

00:02:13.701 --> 00:02:17.850
More positively, the second purpose of it
is, to the extent that our actions during

00:02:17.850 --> 00:02:23.140
the year prevent such over spends occurring,
which of course is our expectation and commitment,

00:02:23.140 --> 00:02:28.700
we will be able to release the 1% for commissioners
to spend accelerating the strategies they

00:02:28.700 --> 00:02:32.300
are currently developing through the STPs
that we have talked about a couple of times

00:02:32.300 --> 00:02:36.450
in the last couple of days and, as we discussed
earlier, of course, in the context of the

00:02:36.450 --> 00:02:38.330
primary care thing.

00:02:38.330 --> 00:02:43.480
So it really is important that we do manage
the risks of over spends occurring both on

00:02:43.480 --> 00:02:47.440
the commissioning side, which I will come
back to later on, and indeed the provider

00:02:47.440 --> 00:02:52.090
side, which we will cover particularly under
the reset.

00:02:52.090 --> 00:02:57.350
Finally on the plan, I just need to point
out that creating that 1% of investment reserve,

00:02:57.350 --> 00:03:02.210
which was not funded separately in the spending
review, has necessitated significantly greater

00:03:02.210 --> 00:03:08.150
ambition by commissioners in the setting of
their plans for efficiency savings in 2016/17,

00:03:08.150 --> 00:03:12.210
which inevitably carries with it a higher
level of risk than we have seen in recent

00:03:12.210 --> 00:03:13.210
years.

00:03:13.210 --> 00:03:15.950
I think I commented on that when we had a
more intensive discussion of the planning

00:03:15.950 --> 00:03:18.060
process at the last meeting.

00:03:18.060 --> 00:03:20.180
So that is the plan.

00:03:20.180 --> 00:03:25.280
The second section, from paragraph 7 onwards,
then sets out the year to date and forecast

00:03:25.280 --> 00:03:26.840
positions for the commissioning sector.

00:03:26.840 --> 00:03:33.450
You will see that both are slightly less than
0.1% of our resource, adverse to plan at this

00:03:33.450 --> 00:03:35.600
stage, for the reasons that I have set out
in the report.

00:03:35.600 --> 00:03:40.690
In the forecast, most of this is technical
in nature.

00:03:40.690 --> 00:03:44.200
We do have a number of CCGs, particularly
in the Midlands and East, who started the

00:03:44.200 --> 00:03:50.400
year behind plan, but we only have two organisations,
Croydon and Shropshire CCGs, who are currently

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:54.130
saying that they cannot recover those positions
and deliver the plans that they have agreed

00:03:54.130 --> 00:03:55.130
with us.

00:03:55.130 --> 00:03:58.840
As you would expect, the relevant regional
teams are very much on the case, working through

00:03:58.840 --> 00:04:03.710
the implications and options with both of
those CCGs.

00:04:03.710 --> 00:04:08.050
Standing back from all of that, of itself
I would advise the Board not to read too much

00:04:08.050 --> 00:04:11.930
into the forecast over spend of £82 million
that results from all of that.

00:04:11.930 --> 00:04:17.269
As I have said, it is technical in nature,
it is less than 0.1% and, perhaps equally

00:04:17.269 --> 00:04:22.160
important, it is less than the £114 million
overspend we were forecasting last year at

00:04:22.160 --> 00:04:23.760
this point.

00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:29.229
So I think those are just important things
to draw your attention to in relation to the

00:04:29.229 --> 00:04:32.289
projection of the financial position.

00:04:32.289 --> 00:04:36.110
Of greater concern is the level of net risk
in the system.

00:04:36.110 --> 00:04:40.599
In the comprehensive risk and mitigation submission
we get from every CCG and direct commissioning

00:04:40.599 --> 00:04:45.940
team every month, this has been assessed,
as I have set out in the report, as £453

00:04:45.940 --> 00:04:50.229
million of risk net of currently identified
mitigations.

00:04:50.229 --> 00:04:53.919
The bulk of the risk comes from the level
of additional stretch in the so called QIPP

00:04:53.919 --> 00:04:56.980
savings plans which I mentioned earlier.

00:04:56.980 --> 00:04:59.090
This is going to require real focus over the
year.

00:04:59.090 --> 00:05:03.360
We are really going to need to watch how those
QIPP plans are developing and, to link back

00:05:03.360 --> 00:05:06.900
to some of the points that Matthew made, we
are going to have to watch the activity trends

00:05:06.900 --> 00:05:09.240
in particular as the year progresses.

00:05:09.240 --> 00:05:15.460
However, I would stress that this is a risk
assessment; it is not a forecast.

00:05:15.460 --> 00:05:20.169
Both we and the CCG community remain completely
committed to delivering within our available

00:05:20.169 --> 00:05:24.879
resources, as we have done every year since
the current commissioning system was formed

00:05:24.879 --> 00:05:26.129
in 2013.

00:05:26.129 --> 00:05:31.819
In that respect, this morning's HSJ bulletin,
which suggests that a significant under spend

00:05:31.819 --> 00:05:35.719
is unlikely - this is absolutely right, for
all the reasons I have stressed on previous

00:05:35.719 --> 00:05:40.660
occasions - but that balance is possible if
we pull out all the stops, is rather closer

00:05:40.660 --> 00:05:45.990
to the mark than yesterday's rather more dire
predictions in the same organ.

00:05:45.990 --> 00:05:48.669
Talking of which, by the way, pulling out
all the stops of course is a good metaphor

00:05:48.669 --> 00:05:53.509
for a public sector body which has more organists
in its senior leadership team than any other

00:05:53.509 --> 00:05:56.130
public body that I know, other than the Royal
College of Organists.

00:05:56.130 --> 00:06:02.000
So there we go, thank you for that personalisation
of the HSJ report.

00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:07.849
Finally, I would just draw your attention
then to the reset process that we have initiated

00:06:07.849 --> 00:06:12.360
in partnership with NHS Improvement, to ensure
that both the commissioning and the provider

00:06:12.360 --> 00:06:15.099
sectors have the best chance of success.

00:06:15.099 --> 00:06:20.449
In this critical first year of the Forward
View period, board members and indeed members

00:06:20.449 --> 00:06:24.840
of the public will doubtless have taken the
time to read the document, absorb its implications,

00:06:24.840 --> 00:06:28.960
and of course we took time in the Commissioning
Committee yesterday to discuss how we can

00:06:28.960 --> 00:06:33.650
best contribute as NHS England and as the
commissioning system to the success of the

00:06:33.650 --> 00:06:37.610
programme of action which I have summarised
at paragraph 21.

00:06:37.610 --> 00:06:40.090
So I wasn't going to go into the detail now.

00:06:40.090 --> 00:06:43.949
I just want to draw out four key messages
on the reset, though.

00:06:43.949 --> 00:06:48.180
The first is, and this is of paramount important,
we have a shared plan.

00:06:48.180 --> 00:06:54.669
A shared plan between NHS England and NHS
Improvement; a shared plan, as it will become,

00:06:54.669 --> 00:06:57.689
with the commissioning sector and the provider
sector at large.

00:06:57.689 --> 00:07:02.400
The second point is, it is going to be extremely
challenging to implement, but it is absolutely

00:07:02.400 --> 00:07:07.430
essential that every single part of the system
delivers its share of the plan.

00:07:07.430 --> 00:07:12.389
Both we and NHS Improvement, with the support
of other national partners including the CQC

00:07:12.389 --> 00:07:16.539
and the Department of Health, are wholly committed
to supporting local health economies to make

00:07:16.539 --> 00:07:22.439
it work, but also to intervening rapidly and
robustly where that appears to be at risk.

00:07:22.439 --> 00:07:27.360
Then the actions that we take over the next
few weeks, and I really mean the next few

00:07:27.360 --> 00:07:34.180
weeks, in particular the provider cost reset
process led by NHS improvement, are particularly

00:07:34.180 --> 00:07:38.740
crucial, both for stabilising the position
this year and for setting us up for success

00:07:38.740 --> 00:07:43.909
as we set out to implement the strategic plans
with developing STPs across the country.

00:07:43.909 --> 00:07:49.219
So it is a plan for success, but it is a plan
that needs to bite quickly, resolutely and

00:07:49.219 --> 00:07:54.189
comprehensively across the country, if we
are going to bring financial discipline to

00:07:54.189 --> 00:07:58.990
the system and deliver in the way I described,
and preserve our capacity to invest behind

00:07:58.990 --> 00:08:06.250
transformation rather than having to use that
resource for covering deficits and other adverse

00:08:06.250 --> 00:08:07.370
variances.

00:08:07.370 --> 00:08:09.150
So that is the core message.

00:08:09.150 --> 00:08:13.969
If I may just slip in a fifth thing which
I forgot to advertise in the little preamble:

00:08:13.969 --> 00:08:17.289
there is one piece of procedural business
that I need to bring to the board for approval

00:08:17.289 --> 00:08:21.909
in connection with the reset and the measures
within it.

00:08:21.909 --> 00:08:28.620
You will have read about the STF, just to
confuse you with another acronym: the Sustainability

00:08:28.620 --> 00:08:36.339
and Transformation Fund, which is covering
both the funding for providers and the funding

00:08:36.339 --> 00:08:37.919
for transformation.

00:08:37.919 --> 00:08:42.320
In particular the provider element of the
Sustainability and Transformation Fund, the

00:08:42.320 --> 00:08:47.290
£1.8 billion which is contingent on them
hitting agreed control totals and performance

00:08:47.290 --> 00:08:52.480
trajectories - linking back to Matthew's point
- we will of course, as the year goes by,

00:08:52.480 --> 00:08:58.000
need to be allocating the £1.8 billion as
earned by trusts to those organisations on

00:08:58.000 --> 00:09:00.490
a rolling basis across the year.

00:09:00.490 --> 00:09:06.220
The board has already agreed the allocation
of the £1.8 billion in the allocation decisions

00:09:06.220 --> 00:09:11.460
we took back in December, and the rules of
operation of the Fund have been published

00:09:11.460 --> 00:09:15.890
by NHSI with our agreement in the last couple
of weeks.

00:09:15.890 --> 00:09:20.990
So the task is largely mechanistic on our
part; it is application of clear rules of

00:09:20.990 --> 00:09:26.210
engagement within a budget which we have already
approved for disbursement.

00:09:26.210 --> 00:09:31.300
So to avoid unnecessary bureaucracy, it would
be helpful for the individual allocations

00:09:31.300 --> 00:09:36.600
to organisations as earned to be delegated
to the Chief Executive and myself, rather

00:09:36.600 --> 00:09:41.510
than requiring us every month or two - or
every quarter, to be more precise - to come

00:09:41.510 --> 00:09:45.040
back to the Board and say, 'Could you please
approve this set of allocations within money

00:09:45.040 --> 00:09:48.140
that we have already earmarked for this purpose?'

00:09:48.140 --> 00:09:53.960
So if you would, Malcolm, put to the board
that particular resolution, it would be helpful

00:09:53.960 --> 00:10:00.180
and avoid delays and bothering both the Investment
Committee and the board with business which

00:10:00.180 --> 00:10:02.760
is not really required.

00:10:02.760 --> 00:10:05.630
That is really all I needed to say; I am happy
to take any questions.

00:10:05.630 --> 00:10:09.970
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: May I take that last point
first, then, which is to invite the board

00:10:09.970 --> 00:10:15.880
to approve it; but on the basis of the rules
having been observed, and there is no payment

00:10:15.880 --> 00:10:19.430
out to those who have failed to deliver their
part of the bargain.

00:10:19.430 --> 00:10:26.080
Are we content to approve that delegation
to the Director of Finance and the Chief Executive

00:10:26.080 --> 00:10:28.820
for the allocation of STF monies?

00:10:28.820 --> 00:10:29.820
Okay.

00:10:29.820 --> 00:10:37.260
Let me then invite any questions to Paul on
other parts of his report.

00:10:37.260 --> 00:10:38.260
No?

00:10:38.260 --> 00:10:41.910
Paul, thank you very much, and thank you for
the clarity.

